B=[("Email — Repositioning Charts","From: Ward Manager","From Monday, repositioning charts must be completed at the bedside at the time the patient is turned, not at the end of the shift. Several charts reviewed last week had been filled in afterwards, with identical entries for the whole night. This is not acceptable: the chart is part of the patient’s legal record.","What is the purpose of the email?",["To introduce a new type of repositioning chart","To correct the way charts are being completed","To explain who may read patients’ charts"],"B"),
("Notice — Medication Fridge","","The temperature of the medication fridge must be checked and recorded twice daily. If the reading is outside the range of 2–8°C, do not use any of the stock. Move the medicines to the spare fridge in the treatment room and telephone Pharmacy immediately.","What must a nurse do if the fridge reading is outside the safe range?",["Adjust the dial and check again in an hour","Record the reading and carry on if the medicines look unchanged","Stop using the stock and contact Pharmacy"],"C"),
("Email — Wound Care Competency Day","From: Nurse Educator","The annual wound care competency day will be held on 14 November. Places are limited to 12, so please sign up by Friday. Staff who completed last year’s full day need only attend the afternoon refresher session.","What does the email say about staff who attended last year?",["They need to attend only the afternoon session","They are not required to take part this year","They must sign up before other staff"],"A"),
("Policy extract — Sharps","","Sharps containers must be replaced when they are three-quarters full. Never press down on the contents and never re-cap needles. The person who uses a sharp is responsible for disposing of it immediately after use.","What does the policy say about used sharps?",["Containers should be emptied once they are full","The user must dispose of them straight away","Cleaning staff are responsible for removing them"],"B"),
("Message from Pharmacy — Patients’ Own Medicines","","A patient’s own medicines must be sent home with the patient or returned to Pharmacy with a completed form. They must never be added to ward stock, even if they are unopened.","What must happen to a patient’s own unopened medicines?",["They must not be used as ward stock","They may be used for other patients if still sealed","They should be destroyed on the ward"],"A"),
("Email — Bedside Handover","From: Nurse Unit Manager","From next week, handover will take place at the bedside for all patients. The exceptions are patients who ask for privacy and situations where sensitive information must be discussed; in these cases handover should be given in the office.","When may handover be given away from the bedside?",["When the ward is very busy","When the patient requests privacy or the information is sensitive","When the patient is asleep"],"B")]
C1=[(7,"According to the first paragraph, why is pain in people with dementia often left untreated?",["People with dementia feel pain less strongly.","Staff are unsure which medicines are safe for them.","Those who cannot describe pain may be assumed not to have it.","The conditions that cause pain are less common in this group."],"C"),
(8,"What does the writer recommend about self-report?",["It should be replaced by observation in all cases.","It should be tried first, using simple scales and repeated questions.","It works best with a numbers scale from zero to ten.","It should be recorded once to avoid confusion."],"B"),
(9,"What does the writer say about observation tools?",["They are most accurate when used while the patient is resting.","They are only suitable for patients who cannot speak.","They are no more reliable than general impressions.","They give more consistent results when staff are trained to use them."],"D"),
(10,"Why does the writer warn against giving sedatives for new agitation?",["They may hide pain without treating it and add to other risks.","They interact badly with simple pain relief.","They are not effective in people with dementia.","They make it harder for staff to assess behaviour."],"A"),
(11,"What is the writer’s main purpose in the fifth paragraph?",["To suggest that relatives should help with personal care","To show that families often overlook signs of pain","To explain why relatives are a useful source of information","To recommend that relatives attend care plan reviews"],"C"),
(12,"What does the writer say about a trial of paracetamol?",["It should be given for one day before further assessment.","A clear improvement afterwards suggests that pain was the cause.","It is unlikely to help if the patient is agitated.","It should be replaced quickly by stronger medicines."],"B"),
(13,"Before considering a psychotropic medicine for an agitated patient, what should a nurse do?",["Ask the family to help calm the patient","Increase the dose of regular analgesia","Check for pain and other treatable causes","Review the patient’s care plan with a doctor"],"C"),
(14,"What is the writer’s overall message?",["Pain in dementia should be actively assessed from several sources rather than assumed absent.","Pain relief is less important than managing behaviour.","Only specially trained staff should assess pain in dementia.","Non-drug measures are enough for most patients with dementia."],"A")]
C2=[(15,"According to the first paragraph, what speeds up the development of antimicrobial resistance?",["The natural ability of bacteria to survive","The overuse and misuse of antibiotics","A rise in deaths from cancer","A lack of new health organisations"],"B"),
(16,"Why are hospitals described as a particular concern?",["Patients there are often unwell, have devices and receive many antibiotics.","Resistant organisms only survive in hospital surroundings.","Staff there use more antibiotics than doctors in the community.","Few hospitals have programmes to control infection."],"A"),
(17,"Why is screening on admission recommended for some patients?",["Hospitals overseas do not report resistant organisms.","Patients may carry resistant organisms without showing symptoms.","Screening reduces the number of antibiotics given.","It identifies patients who need urinary catheters."],"B"),
(18,"What does the writer say about nurses and stewardship?",["They usually lead stewardship programmes.","They decide the dose and duration of antibiotics.","They are well placed to prompt a review of treatment.","They are mainly responsible for isolating patients."],"C"),
(19,"What is the purpose of an ‘antibiotic time-out’?",["To stop all antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours","To check test results before the first dose","To review whether each patient’s antibiotic is still needed and suitable","To teach patients how to take tablets"],"C"),
(20,"Why should cultures be collected before the first antibiotic dose?",["Antibiotics given first may make the results unreliable.","The laboratory can only test samples taken in the morning.","Cultures take 48 to 72 hours to grow.","Doctors need results before prescribing any drug."],"A"),
(21,"What has happened in hospitals that introduced catheter reminder systems?",["Staff have stopped using catheters altogether.","Urinary infections have fallen noticeably.","Isolation precautions have become unnecessary.","Nurses have spent less time on hand hygiene."],"B"),
(22,"What does the writer suggest about educating patients?",["Warnings about resistance are the most effective approach.","Patients should be told to stop antibiotics when they feel better.","Patients rarely understand how antibiotics work.","Clear explanations without blame help patients to cooperate."],"D")]
